---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="Curriculum | GNEDHub">
    <header class="mb-16 max-w-3xl">
        <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-white tracking-tight mb-4">The Connected World</h1>
        <p class="text-slate-400 leading-relaxed font-light">
            Welcome to the hidden wiring of the modern global economy. This four-part curriculum strips away the academic jargon to reveal how the products in your home, the money in your pocket, and the food on your plate are part of a massive, interconnected global machine.
        </p>
    </header>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-2 gap-6 relative">
        
        <!-- Module 1 -->
        <article class="bg-white/[0.03] backdrop-blur-md border border-white/10 rounded-2xl p-8 hover:bg-white/[0.05] hover:border-cyan-500/30 transition-all flex flex-col group">
            <div class="flex items-center justify-between mb-6">
                <span class="text-cyan-400 text-xs font-bold tracking-widest uppercase">Module 01</span>
                <div class="w-16 h-1 bg-white/10 rounded-full overflow-hidden">
                    <div class="w-full h-full bg-cyan-500"></div>
                </div>
            </div>
            <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-white mb-4">The Basics of Economic Globalization & Inequity</h2>
            <div class="space-y-4 text-sm text-slate-400 font-light flex-grow">
                <p>
                    <strong class="text-slate-200 font-medium">The Definition:</strong> According to economist István Benczés (2014), economic globalization is the increasing integration of economies. It is the continuous, cross-border movement of five key elements: goods, services, capital, people, and knowledge.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <strong class="text-slate-200 font-medium">The Inequity:</strong> The global economy is not equally good for everyone. While wealthy nations thrive in the fast lane, poor nations often suffer. This disparity is especially brutal when developing nations are forced to drain their resources to repay massive debts to developed nations.
                </p>
                <div class="pt-4 border-t border-white/5 space-y-2">
                    <p><strong class="text-slate-200 font-medium text-xs uppercase tracking-wider">The Government Ledger</strong></p>
                    <p class="text-xs"><span class="text-cyan-400">Surplus:</span> Collects more in taxes than it spends.</p>
                    <p class="text-xs"><span class="text-red-400">Deficit:</span> Spending exceeds taxes, forcing borrowing.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </article>

        <!-- Module 2 -->
        <article class="bg-white/[0.03] backdrop-blur-md border border-white/10 rounded-2xl p-8 hover:bg-white/[0.05] hover:border-cyan-500/30 transition-all flex flex-col group">
            <div class="flex items-center justify-between mb-6">
                <span class="text-cyan-400 text-xs font-bold tracking-widest uppercase">Module 02</span>
                <div class="w-16 h-1 bg-white/10 rounded-full overflow-hidden">
                    <div class="w-1/2 h-full bg-cyan-500"></div>
                </div>
            </div>
            <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-white mb-4">The Global Assembly Line</h2>
            <div class="space-y-4 text-sm text-slate-400 font-light flex-grow">
                <p>
                    <strong class="text-slate-200 font-medium">Supply Chains:</strong> Foundational, value-adding activities that transform raw materials into finished, usable products.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <strong class="text-slate-200 font-medium">International Production Networks:</strong> No single country builds complex products alone anymore. E.g., airplane manufacturing with parts from Japan, Italy, and the USA.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <strong class="text-slate-200 font-medium">Global Commodity Chains:</strong> Emphasizes the sequence of value-adding chains and highlights the power of global sellers who control massive networks (e.g., oil supply chain).
                </p>
                <p>
                    <strong class="text-slate-200 font-medium">Global Value Chains:</strong> International production sharing on a massive scale—the ultimate extension of division of labor across different countries.
                </p>
            </div>
        </article>

        <!-- Module 3 -->
        <article class="bg-white/[0.03] backdrop-blur-md border border-white/10 rounded-2xl p-8 hover:bg-white/[0.05] hover:border-cyan-500/30 transition-all flex flex-col group">
            <div class="flex items-center justify-between mb-6">
                <span class="text-cyan-400 text-xs font-bold tracking-widest uppercase">Module 03</span>
                <div class="w-16 h-1 bg-white/10 rounded-full overflow-hidden">
                    <div class="w-0 h-full bg-cyan-500"></div>
                </div>
            </div>
            <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-white mb-4">Global Demands & Outsourcing</h2>
            <div class="space-y-4 text-sm text-slate-400 font-light flex-grow">
                <p>
                    <strong class="text-slate-200 font-medium">The Squeeze on Global Demands:</strong> Fierce competition for basic commodities fueled by rapid economic development in previously underdeveloped parts of the world.
                </p>
                <div class="pt-4 border-t border-white/5 space-y-3">
                    <p><strong class="text-slate-200 font-medium">The Mechanics of Outsourcing:</strong> Transferring activities to an outside entity.</p>
                    <ul class="space-y-2 ml-4 list-none border-l border-white/10 pl-4">
                        <li><span class="text-cyan-400 text-xs">Micro-level:</span> Parents utilizing daycares instead of personal caregiving.</li>
                        <li><span class="text-cyan-400 text-xs">Meso-level:</span> A restaurant paying an outside kitchen to prep ingredients.</li>
                        <li><span class="text-cyan-400 text-xs">Macro-level:</span> Corporate delegation, e.g., a British corp sending IT operations to India.</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </div>
        </article>

        <!-- Module 4 -->
        <article class="bg-white/[0.03] backdrop-blur-md border border-white/10 rounded-2xl p-8 hover:bg-white/[0.05] hover:border-cyan-500/30 transition-all flex flex-col group">
            <div class="flex items-center justify-between mb-6">
                <span class="text-cyan-400 text-xs font-bold tracking-widest uppercase">Module 04</span>
                <div class="w-16 h-1 bg-white/10 rounded-full overflow-hidden">
                    <div class="w-0 h-full bg-cyan-500"></div>
                </div>
            </div>
            <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-white mb-4">Consumption & The Race to the Bottom</h2>
            <div class="space-y-4 text-sm text-slate-400 font-light flex-grow">
                <p>
                    <strong class="text-slate-200 font-medium">Hyper-Consumption:</strong> Fueled by a heavy reliance on credit cards to buy things we cannot immediately afford.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <strong class="text-slate-200 font-medium">The Consumption Ecosystem:</strong> Consumers, Consumer Objects/Services, the Consumption Process, and Consumption Sites.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <strong class="text-slate-200 font-medium">Pressure of Incorporation:</strong> The capitalist system constantly needs to expand boundaries, pulling in new markets and consumers (Wallerstein, 1989).
                </p>
                <p>
                    <strong class="text-slate-200 font-medium">Race to the Bottom:</strong> Multinational Corporations hunt for cheapest labor. Less developed countries compete by offering lower wages, poorer conditions, and longer hours, sacrificing their citizens' well-being.
                </p>
            </div>
        </article>

    </div>
</Layout>